<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kadamba dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">345 CE – 540 CE (approx.) · capital Banavasi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first native dynasty of Karnataka, founded by a Brahmin turned warrior, whose reign produced the earliest known Kannada inscription.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talagunda-inscription">Talagunda inscription</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayurasharma">Mayurasharma</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> scholar, was insulted by a Pallava guard while studying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>. He took up arms, gathered forest chiefs and forced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty</a> to recognise him as ruler of the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a> in the mid-4th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayurasharma">Mayurasharma's</a> descendants took the warrior title varma and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banavasi">Banavasi</a>, with a branch at Triparvata. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kakusthavarma">Kakusthavarma</a> made marriage alliances with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a>. The Halmidi inscription, from around 450, is the oldest known inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> remained the main court language. The kings patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jains</a>, and their temple style influenced later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> architecture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by divisions between branches, the Kadambas were conquered around 540 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-i">Pulakeshin I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>. Later families claiming Kadamba descent ruled smaller states, notably the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kadambas-of-goa">Kadambas of Goa</a> and the Kadambas of Hangal.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kadamba-dynasty.jpg" alt="Kadamba dynasty" width="320" height="363"><figcaption>Image: w:Planemad · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Kadamba_Empire_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>345 CE – 540 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Banavasi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mayurasharma, Kakusthavarma, Ravivarma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit, Kannada</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kadambas of Banavasi, Kadambas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=442&amp;focus=kadamba-dynasty" aria-label="Kadamba dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6732 1637 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kadamba dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7052 1893L7052 1893M7052 1893L7188 1956M7052 1893L7097 1795"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7052 1893h0M7188 1956h0M7097 1795h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7052 1893h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7052 1893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Banavasi. <a href="/atlas?year=442&amp;focus=kadamba-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
